Z||000028||Oreif||07-15-2000||02:14 PM||||Rice burners are better built? Aren't most of the japanese cars built in the USA? By the same people who build american cars? I have seen both american cars and foriegn cars that have had "problems" The engineering that goes into the cars may help but it's the people who build them that matters on if a car lasts. (I am not bad mouthing those that assemble them they work hard to meet the demands of the company) You can have a perfectly designed part but odds are 1 of them will have a defect and eventually fail. It's the law of averages on mass produced parts. It all comes down to how well do you take care of your car.||||reg||1
Z||000029||Taijiguy||07-15-2000||05:53 PM||||You touched on a good point Oreif, in that as much as engineering and r&d matter, your product is only as strong as its weakest link. Unfortunately, in the US, that often falls squarely in the lap of the people on the assembly line. Japanese have always had a much stronger work ethic than *most* Americans. The unions (I know this will ruffle some feathers) have give most production workers in the US a freee ride, not making them accountable for their work, by making it almost impossible or too costly for a company to fire a substandard worker. It's the comapanies who have adopted Asian methods of management, organization, pay scales, and training that are producing the best cars. (And in support of the union comment, their contracts here in the US are usually about three pages long, ever see a regular uaw contract? It's like a bible of legal terms and almost as thick!) Saturn pretty much started the trend in using Asian methodology in the US car industry, and their track record speaks for itself. Fortunately, many American car companies (and others) are starting to adopt those same methods. <BR>As for the specifics of the topic, I think the MR2's are pretty cool cars, I've tangled with a couple, and found them fairly competitive on the highway because of their ability to tach out the way they do, but from a rolling start, off the line, and around the corners, there's no competition,  my GT leaves 'em wishing they were at home trying to put bigger exhaust tips on their car! <P>Taiji<P>||||reg||1
Z||000030||Standard||07-16-2000||01:01 AM||||And remember, when Pontiac built the Fiero plant they adopted some of the "eastern philosophy"  I'll try to dig up some of the articles about it.||||reg||4
Z||000031||Oreif||07-16-2000||06:09 AM||||The Fiero was the first car built in a factory that was owned by the the employees.<BR>When the Fiero project ended, the management was offered and started Saturn.||||reg||1
Z||000032||Taijiguy||07-16-2000||10:58 AM||||Hey Raydar, is that a Firenza in that picture? I used to have one of those, what a nice little car. WAY too quick for it's own good (The 231 anyway)<P>-Taiji||||reg||1
Z||000033||Sillhouette.com||07-16-2000||11:19 AM||||Here is my experience with the MR2.<P>I had the chance to change the timing belt on the late model MR2 turbo.  Well let me tell you that the engine had to be lowered. Not only that parts were not readily available.  When I asked the Toyota dealer about any other changes while the engine is that far appart he said no just the timing belt.  Well the pulley had to be changed as well because the timing belt slipped out and rubbed against the timing cover and chewed it up good.  Finding replacement timing covers was a pain in the A$$.  By the time it was done the job costed 1500 dollars and it still did not run like a fiero.  I took it for test drives and cornering and could not break the end loose or get that wonderfull G's that I get from my Fiero.  I have never seen one of these on the Solo track yet however I have met up to one on the Highway. Beat it on the straight away and than again beat it on the off ramp and I was on the outside lane.  Maybe they can outhandle the non modified suspension Fiero but if you lower your ride and add some KYB's the MR2 has no chance.  <BR>The car that I worked on had Eibach springs with Koni adjustables greedy turbo upgrade and timer with borla exhaust and 17" Einkei rims Nittos as rubber.  It looked good but only as a coffe table.<P>Cheers<P>P.S. before you go thinking that I don't know how to change timing belts I owned a 1993 Eagle Talon TSI and I still on a 1992 GTP twin dual cam.  ||||reg||1
Z||000034||ur2biteme||07-17-2000||03:36 AM||||There's another thing too.  if you realistically look at it, the 4cyl is kind of in the same general arena as the non turbo mr2s.  I have the 4cyl with a flowmaster, splitfires, k&n, a removed air intake hose and the broken a/c stuff out of the way (some other little things I can't remember too...) and I know how to drive the car.  <P>Unless these damn ricers actually do some decent mods, I BEAT them in drag races, cornering, and LEAVE them on the freeway.  <P>I'm not b.s.ing you either.  I can't tell you how many accords got to see my fender hole get smaller and smaller in front of them.......after I see that damn giant, hard to miss, autometer tach sitting somewhere around 6500 and climbing......and I still get 30-35mpg average, with my lead foot.<P>Besides, that half litre larger AMERICAN motor tuned similarly to the ricers still sounds american.  Still growling, not whining, at 165k.    <IMG SRC="http://www.fiero.nl/forum/smile.gif"><P>P.S.  notice that the MR2 came out the next year from the highly successful Fiero?  Coincidence?  I think not.<BR><p>[This message has been edited by ur2biteme (edited 07-17-2000).]||||reg||1
Z||000035||slofiero||12-06-2000||07:50 PM||||My MR-2 (non-turbo) will handle better than all my other vehicles (the subaru doesn't stand a chance), but not in my hands.  I am not a bad driver (I believe that I am pretty good) but the MR-2 is very twitchy (unstable) when being pushed to the limits, the Fiero instills more confidence.<BR>My list of Fieros includes:  87 SE 4 cyl 5 speed, 86 GT auto, 87 SE V-6 5 speed, 87 GT 5 speed (never got it running and I just traded it) and I have an 86 SE V-6 4 speed on the way.<BR>I have driven the 1st gen MR-2 as well and did not like it much (better than the 4 cyl fiero,performance wise) overall.  I personally prefer a more stable feeling and the 'feeling' of more weight.  My V-6 fieros will remain my car of choice as they smoke the MR-2 in acceleration and I can handle the Fiero better than the MR-2 as I like to remain firmly planted on the road.  I know that my 87 SE would do about 140-145 m.p.h., converted from 5100 r.p.m., and the 91 MR-2 stops at 118 (long straights and plenty of time).<BR>As for racing, a Navy sailor I know (through E-mail) races his 86 GT and won the last event he was able to participate in, in his class (C stock).  You can view the results by going to SCCA.org and tracing to his region (Old Dominion)or go to: <BR> <A HREF="http://www.widomaker.com/~sanetrik/solo/Solo_072300.htm" TARGET=_blank>http://www.widomaker.com/~sanetrik/solo/Solo_072300.htm</A> <P>Look for Jassen Bowman.  If you do not believe that the MR-2 is better for performance (in stock trim) then look at their classifications by the SCCA, who divides them by performance potential.<BR> <A HREF="http://www.scca.org/amateur/solo2/classes/java/index.html" TARGET=_blank>http://www.scca.org/amateur/solo2/classes/java/index.html</A> <BR> <A HREF="http://www.scca.org/amateur/club_racing/classes/database.html" TARGET=_blank>http://www.scca.org/amateur/club_racing/classes/database.html</A> <P>Always remember that the driver controls the machine.<P>------------------<BR>Thomas Martell<BR>91 MR-2<BR>86 Fiero<BR>80 TR-7<BR>74 914/1.8<BR>78 Subaru 4wd||||reg||1
Z||000036||PearlBlueSoul||12-06-2000||08:58 PM||||WOW that page was FULL of misconceptions on BOTH sides.  wow...I bout lost it.  Wished I coulda been allowed to post on that thread.  these guys need a big fat history lesson.<P>The Deuce is not ALWAYS an easy car to work on.  any more than any other mid engine car is.  my first gen is ok, but even it has it's moments where I go into cussing fits.  and I know it's no different in a fiero (did an engine replacement in a 4 banger SE, whoa that was interesting).  <P>As far as they go tho, I don't really mind having either one.  I've ridden in a fiero now so I know what they feel like in comparison to mine.  like a mini vette on the inside:  you're very low, when modified they're rock solid, and the binders are very good.  Liked it alot.  But, their reliability holds me back on getting one.  I can't have a car that's not reliable at my age.  just don't have the $$$ to keep one running.<P><BR>Lenny:  you smoked your brother in law's deuce?  what year/version does he have, and what version fiero do you have?  if you beat a first gen with a SE fiero, unless the MR2 was auto and you were stick I can't believe that.  if it was a V6 versus S/C then I could, if he were a bad driver.  Most of the time it can be all driver that wins a race.  Hell, I know I've beaten some stuff I prolly shouldn't have, but I don't think the guys new how to drive their cars, and I do.  <P>all this only to say that it comes down to what feel you like the most.  I have to say I like the Go kart-ness of my MR2 more than the vette/F-body-ness of a Fiero.  But that's just my peronal preference.  Got nothin on Fieros, I think they're fine!  The looks are good (that will grow on you with time.  you get partial to it, so I see why you guys like them more than 2s.  I've had 2 MR2s so I'm more apt to like how my car looks over a fiero), the mods are great, and from what I've ridden in and read about them they don't sound half bad.  If I really knew hard facts about their maintenence I might consider getting the other 2 tone on this board and putting it beside my Deuce.  <P>As my mechanics teacher put it:  Opinion's are like assholes-  they're all dirty to someone else, they all stink to someone else, and eveyone has one.<BR><P>------------------<BR>1987 Toyota MR2 N/A 5 speed<BR>  <A HREF="http://www.atlantamr2s.com" TARGET=_blank>http://www.atlantamr2s.com</A>  <BR><A HREF="http://www.geocities.com/darionscard" TARGET=_blank>http://www.geocities.com/darionscard</A>  <BR>    <IMG SRC="http://www.geocities.com/darionscard/Thumb.jpg">       <BR><B>Notable mods:</B>  Sus tech springs and sway bars, Toyota strut tower brace, KYB strut inserts, HKS exhaust, NGK plugs/wires, Momo Racer wheels (14x6), Custom intake, Kenwood 12" speaker in front trunk, Jensen amp.||||reg||1
Z||000037||PearlBlueSoul||12-06-2000||09:00 PM||||Oh, if you wanna hear what a MR2 sounds like, and disprove that stereotype of a ricey pipe, goto my web site.  I have WAV files there of both S/C and n/A MKIs.||||reg||1
Z||000038||GarethWright||12-06-2000||10:05 PM||||I once beat a Supra Turbo away from a stop light in my sisters 95 Cavalier 4-banger!  But Only cause He didn't know we were racing   <IMG SRC="http://www.fiero.nl/forum/smile.gif"><P>erm, just realized that this has absolutely nothing to do with this thread, but there ya go  <IMG SRC="http://www.fiero.nl/forum/smile.gif"><p>[This message has been edited by GarethWright (edited 12-06-2000).]||||reg||1
Z||000039||BigBoyToys||12-06-2000||11:41 PM||||slofiero,<BR>Welcome to the forum!<BR>But this may not have been the best choice for your first post in light of recent events. But good to see you started in the Archives, certianly a great source of technical information.<BR>But enjoy! It really is a GREAT group of Fiero enthusiast.<BR>BBTs||||reg||6
Z||000040||ArmandoP||12-07-2000||05:08 AM||||geez people, I thought that other site had a bunch of misconceptions!!!<BR>here are some myths that are just wrong.  (not flaming, just letting you know)<BR>the mr2 did in fact come in Japan as an 84 model year.  Lotus did have a hand in the design...atleast lotus emplyees did.<BR>cars are cars...reliability is simply an issue of owner maintenance.<BR>most races are won by the driver....Ive beat mustang 5.0's and my roomates transam WS6 in my puny little 1.6 liter mr2 when I had a slipping clutch!  does that mean my car is faster than theirs???? hell no, just that the 5.0 guy couldnt drivbe...and yeah, he knoew we were racing, and I pulled hard. we talked afterwards....<BR>anyways, now I know how you guys must have felt when you showed up on the mr2 site and people were spewing out all kinds of BS about your cars that arent true!<BR>more myths...the engine in the first gen MR2 is a 4age...same engine toyota uses in their atlantic cars...capable of 240 horses all motor..NA!  not bad for a 1.6...the one in the MKII turbos you ask?   3sgte...same engine in rod millens pikes peak truck....capable of over 450 hp with merely bolt ons.  its a 2 liter.  no these are not stock figures, but an engine capable of that kind of power without opening it up has to be worth SOMETHING.<BR>and please...I dont compare your car to a chevette, dont compare mine to an accord or a civic. :-) MR2's ANd fieros are sports cars...civic, accords and chevettes are not.<P>looks are purely subjective....except for in the case of the toyota echo and the pontiac Aztek.   <IMG SRC="http://www.fiero.nl/forum/smile.gif"><P>Im going to say the same thing that fiero people said when they came over to our board because its the only thing that makes sense to say.<BR>We have mid engined rear drive 2 seaters of the 80's.  our cars are WAY too similar to have this bickering among us.  why dont we get over all the BS and pride and learn from each other? Hell, Im hosting a track day at gingerman raceway park and any fiero that wants to come out is more than welcomed to....theres another thread around here about it.<BR>Ive driven fieros and (obviously) MR2's  you can take your guess at which one I liked more.   <IMG SRC="http://www.fiero.nl/forum/smile.gif">  fact is, we live in a country where its YOUR choice what you drive.  no point in disrespecting others choices...once again, unless its an echo or an aztek. :P<BR>There are Fieros out there...probably many, that will whoop my ass in the quarter, and probably in the twisties as well. There are things I like about Fieros...the v-6 exhaust note is mean.  the 88GT's look great.  its EASIER than a NY hooker to drop the engine. (Ive done both an MR2 and a fiero 4 cyl.)  <BR>Why did I choose the MR2?  shifter thats smooth as butter, engine that revs into the stratosphere, and it seemed like every where I looked I saw a Fiero. There are other things I like about the fiero (Ive driven 2 V-6ers, near perfect condition, and 3 4 bangers 2 in nasty shape)<P>heres my solution...come out to gingerman with us this summer.  dont bring an attitude and throw away your misconceptions (Im asking the same of MR2 owners) of the other cars.  dont worry about making it a competition, cause its not.  enjoy yourself, learn about each others cars, eat some BBQ, drive on the track in the car that you love. (or just hang out, you dont have to run the track to go)  I guarantee you will leave with a different feeling towards MR2 and MR2 owners who are enthusiasts...not the 40 year old women who bought them cause theyre cute(c'mon, you know the same ****  happened with the fiero!)   <IMG SRC="http://www.fiero.nl/forum/biggrin.gif">  I can also guarantee that the MR2 owners will leave with a much better idea of what the fiero is all about.  come hang with us, and we can all get along...kinda like hippies but without the flower power. hehehe<P>email me if you want more info on the gingerman event. it is hosted by IMOC, the international Mr2 Owners Club, Illiana chapter, but all cars (that wont triple our insurance rate) are welcomed! (cars that cannot run becauseof insurance problems are vipers and f-bodies, thats all I know of so far.)<BR>hope to see you guys there!  <BR>Armando<BR>mid-engine all the way!<BR>please excuse spelling errors, its 5 am...all nighter tonight!<P>------------------<BR>__________________<BR>will work for MR2 parts!!! <BR>1987 MR2 NA t-bar csp with 700 miles on its new engine. <BR>TRD copper head gasket, milled head .020, 2 short shifters,metal masters rear brake pads, 195-60-14 (soon to get new tires), clear corners, Clevite 77 bearings, NO A/C, MAF relocation mod, red silicon vacuum hoses, redline tranny fluid,and the stock exhaust with a WHOLE bunch of broken welds! <BR>indiana/illinois MR2 owners club<BR>[URLhttp://php.indiana.edu/~ajperez/inimoc.html[/URL]<BR>     <IMG SRC="http://php.indiana.edu/~ajperez/mytwo.jpg">  <p>[This message has been edited by ArmandoP (edited 12-07-2000).]||||reg||1
